<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! Seat harness ~ a capability never lent by ambient authority

A seat holds only the capabilities its wall grants. Every act runs under exactly one authority, and the harness names it. Each lineage (Codex, Copilot, Claude) wears the same seat spec, so a wall keeps one meaning across them. The crew shapes and the lane law stand at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/mixed-model-swarm`>>; this carrier holds the machinery beneath them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-trust-model`>>
!! The trust model ~ who holds each capability, whose authority runs each act

|! Actor |! Holds |! Runs under its own authority |! Never |
| ''Operator'' | rulings, credentials, the push of the span and `main`, every founding and custody act | anything | delegates a credential to a seat |
| ''Controller'' | the one go per push, seat assignment, the lane's merge, the read of every chunk | its own commits and pushes | executes code from a lane tree it has not read |
| ''Broker'' (`tools/commit-relay.mjs`) | fixed data acts a lead requests: a commit of named paths, a push the controller's go names, a builder worktree, a walled seat's launch | git and the launcher, from a path outside the lane | grants a go; launches a lead; touches a path outside the lane root |
| ''Lead'' | its worktree and its lane root, no git dir and no network flag (the model API rides the codex process, outside the sandbox) | edits, tests, briefs, requests to the broker | commits or pushes itself; writes the main checkout |
| ''Builder'' | its worktree alone | edits, typecheck, single-file tests | commits; reaches the network |
| ''Reader'' | nothing writable | reads | writes any file; its final message carries the hand-back |

''The trust boundary of the broker.'' The broker executes no code from a lead's tree. It runs data-only git plumbing (`write-tree`, `commit-tree`, `update-ref` with an expected old head) through a pinned absolute `git`, a fixed environment (attributes read from the empty tree), an isolated home and temp dir, and a push that runs from a throwaway repo of the broker's own to the controller's remote URL, so the lane's `.git/config` (pushurl, insteadOf, helpers) is never read, so a planted hook, credential helper, `.gitattributes` filter, submodule, poisoned `PATH`, `GIT_*` or `NODE_OPTIONS` reaches nothing. Hooks and validators run in a separate ''check seat'' behind bubblewrap (no network, a read-only root, one writable work dir) over an exact export of the tree; the broker commits only when it verifies that seat's signed receipt, which covers the tree, the check names and the check definitions; the checked tree is bound read-only for every check, and the key and the receipts live in the broker's memory alone, never on disk, because a workspace-write sandbox fences writes, not reads, and it refuses a hardlinked file. Installs run in an ''install seat'' that binds the worktree, the store, the git dir and every protected path by bubblewrap's fd binds (`--bind-fd`, `--ro-bind-fd`) over directories held open once, and reads `.npmrc` through the held worktree handle with O_NOFOLLOW (a symlink there refuses by name before any read) (scripts off, pnpmfile ignored, offline, a refused `.npmrc` key by name, `.git` read-only, the controller dir read as empty so the key and the go stay unreadable). The proofs live in `tools/commit-relay.test.mjs`. The request directory is held open by handle (`/proc/self/fd` anchoring) so a swapped `relay` redirects nothing. A new worktree is built where it stays, in a broker-owned staging directory, and the lane shows it as a symlink the broker makes through a parent directory it holds open, with rollback through the same handle. The trusted launcher must sit outside the scratch tree entirely, and the broker's own state dir is checked by real path: outside the lane, every worktree and the whole scratch lane tree, before ANY directory (relay/ included) is created. Two edges remain open: the receipt key lives in the broker process's memory, so a same-user `ptrace` of the broker would read it (the host's `ptrace_scope` and the seat's seccomp are the wall there), and the wall rests on `bwrap` being present (its absence refuses by name, never runs unwalled).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-seat-spec`>>
!! The seat spec ~ one shape, rendered per lineage

A seat names its lineage, model, effort, mode, wall, seat directory under `~/.lares-scratch/<lane>/`, and its brief file. `tools/launch-codex.mjs` renders it into Codex flags and `tools/launch-copilot.mjs` into Copilot tool gates.

|! Mode |! Writes |! Network |! Commits |
| `read` | nothing (the final message carries the hand-back) | off | never |
| `research` | its seat directory | on, with search | never |
| `build` | its worktree and seat directory | off | never; its lead lands the diff |
| `lead` (Codex) | its worktree, the repo's git dir, its lane root | on | through the broker alone |

''Walls that hold by measure.'' The Codex sandbox keeps `.git` read-only inside every granted root, so no sandboxed Codex seat commits. A seat launched inside another seat's sandbox inherits that sandbox and cannot initialize its own runtime state, so the broker launches every seat a lead asks for. A Copilot build seat's tool gate may deny `node` and `pnpm`; its lead or the controller then runs the tests.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-lead`>>
!! The lead ~ fresh per step, never one long session

* `tools/lane-state.mjs` holds the shape and `node tools/launch-codex.mjs --lead-step --lane-root <dir> --model <m> --effort <e> --worktree <wt>` launches the step: it reads `<lane-root>/state.json`, writes `<lane-root>/steps/step-NNN/brief.md` from the state, and launches a lead there with the lane root added so the lead can rewrite the state. The step counter spends only when the preflight passes, a missing or misshapen state refuses by name, and the lead ceiling bounds the step.
* A lead runs ONE step per launch: it reads its lane's state file (head, owed rows, live seats, worktree map), does that step, writes the state file and its hand-back, and stops. The next step launches a fresh lead over the new state.
* A step stays short: about 10 to 15 minutes or 40 tool calls. An agentic session re-sends its whole transcript on every call, so one long session grows its draw roughly with the square of its length.
* A lead dispatches and waits outside the model: the broker or the controller's watcher blocks on a seat, and the next lead step reads the result path. A lead never polls inside its own context.
* A failing test counts as work. A lead stops only at a wall, at a design question that needs an operator ruling (named with its options), or at the push go.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-broker`>>
!! The broker ~ the hands outside a walled lead

* ''The swap'': `tools/seat-broker.mjs` holds the plumbing doors, `tools/seat-walls.mjs` the check and install seats, `tools/tree-export.mjs` the filter-free tree export. ''Requests'' arrive as `req-<id>.json` files in `<lane-root>/relay/`, zero-padded and in order, one at a time; each answer lands as `res-<id>.json` before the next request runs. Unknown kinds and unknown fields refuse by name.
* ''Doors'': `commit` (named relative paths read race-proof from the worktree, the message as inline text (size-capped; the broker opens no lead-controlled file for it), an expected old head, a verified check receipt; no hook, no filter); `push` (only when the go file names the branch AND the head; never `-u`, never force; the go spends on the attempt); `worktree` (a new path under the lane root, a branch carrying the lane prefix, a filter-free export of the base tree, submodule paths left empty); `install` (the install seat over a worktree in the lane root); `worktree-remove` (a registered, clean tree, never the broker's own, never `--force`); `launch` (`build`, `read` or `research` alone, never `lead`; allowlisted models; a seat directory inside the lane root with its brief).
* ''The go'' lives outside the lane root and the worktree, at a path the controller names when it starts the broker, so a lead cannot grant its own push.
* ''The broker runs'' from a copy outside the lane and with a launcher outside the lane; a lead's edit to the lane's own copy never reaches the running broker.
* ''A crew cap'' may ride the broker: it refuses a `launch` while a seat of the same kind stays live, naming the cap, so a lead adapts by reading the answer.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-spend-guard`>>
!! The spend guard ~ true usage, read where each tool writes it

* ''True usage'' reads from each Codex rollout's `token_count` events (`total_token_usage`: input, cached input, output, reasoning) and from each rollout's `rate_limits.primary.used_percent`. A seat's printed "tokens used" line understates the draw by orders of magnitude, so no tally reads it.
* ''Cached input counts.'' Caching lowers the price of a token, never its weight against a rate limit or a plan pool.
* ''The floors'': the Codex pool keeps 20% for a build, research or lead seat and 0% for a read seat; the launcher refuses at or under the floor.
* ''The used-percent lines'': a lead step refuses above 50% of the weekly pool used, a build or research seat above 60%, and a read seat stays at its floor; each refusal names the reading.
* ''The per-seat ceiling'': the launcher starts a detached supervisor that is the seat's parent: it spawns codex itself, so the child pid cannot be reused before the supervisor reaps it, and it signals only its own child. It tails the seat's own rollout (matched by the seat's own marker, which rides its prompt and which the first user-prompt event of its rollout records; a seat with no NEW token event for 200 ticks (ten minutes; a re-read of the last total does not count; `--blind-ticks N` moves the limit and the default stays 200) is stopped, and any error while watching stops it too, failing closed. The stop writes ceiling.md FIRST, then runs the ladder (SIGTERM to the group, grace, SIGKILL to the group, a wait for the exit event); a signal that throws never reads as stopped, and a watch that ends with the child alive makes the supervisor SIGKILL its own child. The relay's go and state live under the SAME fixed broker root, `<root>/go/<lane-id>/push-go` and `<root>/iso/<lane-id>`, where the lane id is the lane's base name plus a digest of its REAL path (two lanes sharing a base name never share a go or a state dir). The controller's `tools/push-go.mjs` writes a four-line go (lane id, branch, head, push destination) and the relay refuses a go whose lane or destination differs from the one it holds. No caller names a path (a go or state dir anywhere under `~/.lares-scratch`, another lane included, refuses); a made worktree stages in `.broker-worktrees/<lane-id>` under scratch (verified like the root: real directories, ours, exactly 0700), no granted root (worktree, seat dir, lane root) may equal, contain or sit inside that staging tree except the exact made worktree a build seat was handed, and a build or lead worktree outside scratch refuses. A launched seat runs in a BROKER-OWNED seat directory under that ONE fixed broker root that no caller names: `~/.local/state/lares-broker` with `seats`, `go` and `iso` inside it (moved only by the `LARES_BROKER_ROOT` environment the controller sets), outside `~/.lares-scratch` entirely, made 0700, verified by lstat on every component from the home down (real directories, ours, no group- or world-writable ancestor, the root exactly 0700), with a size bound (`LARES_BROKER_MAX_GB`, ten by default) that refuses a launch by name, a fresh `mkdtemp` seat dir per launch, and the seat dir removed after the hand-back or a refusal. A dry run creates nothing. Codex runs inside a PID namespace (`bwrap --unshare-pid --die-with-parent`, measured to nest with Codex's own sandbox on this host), so when the namespace's init dies every descendant dies, setsid or not; a live seat refuses by name where that wall cannot stand. Every supervision exit path (a throw, a clean exit, an unstopped group, a size stop) ends in one `finally` that removes the broker seat dir, or keeps it and names it in the marker while its group still lives, and a running seat's seat dir has a live byte bound (`LARES_SEAT_MAX_BYTES`, two GiB by default; over it the seat stops with verdict `size-ceiling`). The namespace contains every descendant but does not reserve the host process-group id after the child is reaped; that pid-reuse edge stays accepted. The launch message names a completion marker `done-<uuid>` the supervisor writes LAST (it carries the verdict, the exit code, the names copied and any hand-back error, and it stands even when the supervision itself fails; the wait also watches the supervisor's pid), a containment command that fails before Codex runs reads `setup-failed`, and every group signal rides one guard, `killGroup`, that refuses any pgid that is not an integer above 1 (a pid-1 test fake once reached `kill(-1)` and killed every process the operator owns; signal-spawning suites run only inside a bwrap PID namespace, and a witness reads any `process.kill` outside the guard as red), and a reused lane seat dir moves its old final.md, ceiling.md, out.md and done-* into `prev-<n>/` first. Under that root: Codex's `-o final.md`, `TMPDIR`, run.log and ceiling.md all land there, where no lead can write, and the lead names a lane seat dir the broker never runs a writer against. After the seat's process group is gone, the supervisor copies the seat's regular top-level files (each capped at 8 MiB, at most 64, never symlinks or directories) into the lane's seat dir through a held handle (an old entry unlinked, then O_NOFOLLOW|O_EXCL), so a lane dir swapped mid-run redirects nothing. Every launcher write into the lane's seat dir (the brief, attempts.log, the snapshot, the marker, the prompt) goes through one directory handle opened and verified under the lane, with O_NOFOLLOW and a single-link check; lead steps hold the lane root the same way (`steps/`, the claim and `state.json` all through it); the supervisor inherits both handles as fds. A witness test reads any pathname fs write in `launch-codex.mjs` or `lane-state.mjs` as red, and an access witness reads ANY fs call on a non-held path in the four broker files (`commit-relay.mjs`, `seat-broker.mjs`, `seat-walls.mjs`, `tree-export.mjs`) as red unless it goes through a held handle or carries the broker-owned mark (the controller's own arguments, the broker root, the staging tree, the home's credential list). The staging parent's verifier also refuses a group- or world-writable scratch dir or home. When a child exits, the supervisor SIGKILLs its whole process group at once (ESRCH is fine) and confirms the group answers ESRCH before a stop reads as stopped) and sums `total_token_usage.total_tokens`; at the ceiling (15M for a lead step, 8M for build, read and research; `--ceiling N` overrides) it sends SIGTERM, then SIGKILL, and writes `ceiling.md` in the seat directory naming the reading. The watchdog never reads `run.log`. A Copilot run reads its spend from its log's `Requests N Premium` line, and a log without one reads UNMETERED, never zero.
* ''Compaction'': every seat launches with `-c model_auto_compact_token_limit=120000` and `-c tool_output_token_limit=8000`, launcher overrides only (`~/.codex/config.toml` stays the operator's). `--canary` (read mode) writes a brief that makes about 100 small tool calls in one turn and runs under a 50000 limit; `--check-canary --seat-dir D` reads that seat's rollout and prints `COMPACTION-FIRED` (exit 0) or `COMPACTION-NOT-FIRED` (exit 1) with the event, call and token counts; the launcher snapshots the existing rollouts before it spawns the seat. Measured 2026-11-04 (Luna, low effort): compaction fired inside `codex exec` (1 event, about 100 calls, 2.9M total tokens).
* ''Reasoning effort'' moves little of the draw; context length moves most of it. Effort follows the step's difficulty, never the budget.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-swarm-controls`>>
!! The swarm's controls ~ signed tiddlers in the daemon wiki, at standing

The swarm's live controls rest in the live daemon wiki as signed records, verified when read. The operator ruled it at standing, built before the founding (2026-11-04): capability, not account; identity-as-relation; no key on a surface and no key file a seat can read. The flat go file and the relay's in-memory HMAC key retire as the later slices land.

| Control | Signed by, as relation | Writer |
| the house cap (a line on the face-private board) | the operator's face, by hand | the hand only (a hand-only verb, surface origin) |
| a lane's share (a line on the face-private board) | the face by hand, or the controller under a cap the hand granted | hand / controller |
| `$:/lares/swarm/go/<branch>` | the face by hand, or the controller under a granted cap | binds branch and head; spent on the attempt |
| `$:/lares/swarm/lane/<lane>/state` | the lead, under its own seat name | the lead |
| `$:/lares/swarm/roster/<seat>` | the VESSEL (the daemon's device to face relation), as witness | the watchdog reports; the daemon signs; never the measured lead |
| `$:/lares/swarm/receipt/<tree>` | the VESSEL, same | the broker asks after the walled checks pass; the daemon signs |

A lead may add note rows beside its roster rows under its own seat name; it never signs spend. The house cap and the lane shares rest on the face-private promote board, never as daemon-wiki titles: their `$:/lares/swarm/house-cap` and `lane/<lane>/share` titles name the family, and no record rests there. A lead's trust binds its seat, its lane and its key together, so a lead signs its own lane's state alone.

* ''The model stands'' (`swarm-tiddlers`, `swarm-board`). Every record signs under the `swarm-act` domain, its own name, so no swarm record verifies as a promote or kahua act. A read checks shape, domain, face group, signature, the title the body belongs at and the signer's role; any failure reads as absent, so a read fails closed. The house cap and the lane shares ride the face-private promote board as a sibling record family (they cross devices: the whole swarm answers to one cap); each line folds by causal heads and concurrent heads take the minimum, so a fork only tightens the swarm. A line that cites a parent it lacks, or holds no head, reads zero and UNAVAILABLE by its reason (`parent-missing`, `no-head`): `swarm-show` names it, and a write onto it refuses `swarm-line-unavailable` unless it is a hand's explicit `supersede`, which cites the line's dangling heads and starts the line again. A torn record enters the refused, never silence. The board's integrity rests on who can write the face's board doc and the daemon doc: verify-on-read refuses a forged record, but a writer of those docs can still drop records or tear them, which reads as an unavailable line. Shares never sum past the cap: the write refuses it, and a board whose shares sum past the cap folds every share to zero. A go binds a branch and a head, carries a nonce, and is spent once: the vessel claims one spend record per go atomically, so racing spenders land one. Go, lane state, roster and receipts rest in the daemon document, device-local by its anchor.
* ''Off disk.'' The `$:/lares/swarm/` prefix reads as control plane (`isControlPlaneUri`) and never projects (`neverProjects`), under every spelling that starts with it or resolves beneath it, a dot segment that folds out of it included; each record also carries `disk-projection: "no"`. A name refuses before it can spell such an escape: no swarm name, decoded or not, holds an empty, `.` or `..` segment, a one-segment name (lane, seat, tree, spend) holds no `/`, and a go's branch keeps git's ref-name rules (`git check-ref-format`).
* ''The verbs.'' `swarm-show` reads (open to a seat); `swarm-cap-set`, `swarm-share-set` and `swarm-go-issue` are hand-only (`swarm-needs-hand`), at the socket and on the daemon island. They sign through `SwarmSignerPort`, wired to the ward's signer (#/the-ward): each signing row opens the face root fresh under the route typed for it, so a script holding unlocked custody signs none. The `who` check stays advisory for `show` alone.
* ''Before the wiki stands'', every swarm act refuses by name: "no standing daemon: swarm controls need the founded wiki". There is no flat-file fallback.
* ''Still to land'': the co-sign of a second device for the house cap and the push go (S3); the capability channel and the unix-socket probe (S4); the vessel-signed roster and receipt, the broker swap and the retirement of the flat go and the HMAC key (S5). The go's spend and the vessel's claim are modelled (`spendGo`, `trust.vessel`) and stay unwired until S5. A two-device witness that swarm tiddlers stay device-local, a live probe of the store-door rows against a founded face, and a probe of whether a seat's `meme-put` or a synced tiddler can reach the daemon doc's `$:/lares/swarm/` titles stay owed.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-ward`>>
!! The ward ~ the face root opens fresh for one hand act, never from unlocked custody

The operator ruled it (2026-11-04): users keep full caps over their own wikis, while the daemon wiki and the kahua stage are bound by the ward, their startup actions included. Never a passkey or WebAuthn: that binds the house to a web2.5 registry.

* ''The key.'' Each face holds a WARD key in its own slot tree (`faces/<fcid>/ward-slots.bin`, a floor row), bound over the face's human routes (a passphrase, a paper pin) and never a passkey (`ward-passkey-refused`, at bind, in a hand-edited tree, and as a credential). The face root seed rests `ward-cold`: sealed under the ward key in the ward's own frame (`lares-ward-seal`, domain `custody-carrier-ward`), never under the face key, so the census faults an FCK-framed root where a warded one belongs.
* ''One act, one unlock.'' No door of unlocked custody reads, writes, moves or removes the root (`ward-needs-fresh-unlock`). A ward act opens the ward key fresh from the route typed for it, through the minted KDF port, spends the face's own unlock attempts on a miss, signs, and zeroes the key; the key never enters the faces the holder holds open, and nothing caches a route or a key between acts.
* ''Where the route comes from.'' The CLI reads it from a TTY alone (`ward-needs-tty` refuses an environment variable and a piped stdin). It never rides a store verb's arguments (those hash into a receipt): it rides one custody frame bound to the act it names (its verb, its face and a digest of its arguments), and comes back as a one-use ref the verb carries; a ref spent on any other act refuses and burns. No module holds a route between acts: the unlock hands its route to the composition it stands (its boot dial alone), and a take to the re-present it asks for, each as an argument. A sealed act carries it in its own `ward` argument. A founding rides the route its face is born under. The node panel carries none (its verbs ride the daemon document), so its bound acts refuse by name toward the terminal. A browser signs in a per-act ward worker, fresh from the passphrase its page dialog collects, three misses per page. No file answers for a route or a new face's route: a test harness seats its own explicitly (`tools/test-ward-route.mjs`, in its CLI processes alone).
* ''The bound acts.'' Every act that reads the root: a daemon or kahua `promote-commit` (its ledger row carries `wardSig` over (wiki, digest, seq), and boot voids a bound row without one, however the row reached the ledger), `kapae-lower`, the swarm's house cap, lane shares and push go, and every act that changes who belongs to the face or what it trusts: admit, fleet-join, recovery contacts, the handles, nexus publish, the lease, the intent root, a holder's edge kāpae, the seal witness, backup and restore, the founder's certificate at a new scope. `kapae-raise` only adds a brake, so it fails safe and signs as the device's own atom.
* ''A face founded before the ward'' refuses `ward-absent` until `lares vault rewrap --face N` binds its ward and moves its root under it (the tree first, so a torn rewrap resumes).

Residuals, named: the ward is only as strong as the passphrase, offline; the browser root still rests under the keystore's tree key (only its bound acts sign through the ward worker), and its page hands the typed passphrase to the worker; the founder certificate at a new scope issues inside the face-unlock act, under the route the person just typed; the kahua lift, withdraw and carry sign with the root and so ride the ward, though the ruling names every lift free (freeing them needs a device-atom lift in the kahua reader, the operator's call); a racer that reads a ward ref off the daemon document can spend it only on the identical act the operator asked for (binding it to the socket waits on S4's channel); a dropped bound promotion leaves a tombstone in the same device-local ledger row the face's custody writes, so ANY plaintext edit by a writer holding unlocked custody can strip that tombstone and replay the dropped row, not only a whole-row rollback; the cure (a floor that writer cannot forge: a board-anchored drop shadow, or a ward-signed ledger head) stands owed after standing as the road's `L23`, and lands with no re-genesis since the ledger is device-local; the co-sign of a second device (S3) gates the lower, the house cap and the push go (`S3b`, #/the-swarm-slices).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/owed`>>
!! Owed ~ the rows this design still lacks

# A deep read and debug pass over the whole harness once the wiki server stands (the Terra rounds continue there; the nine rounds so far found their classes by placement and authority, and the residuals named below stand open until then: the host process-group id after a reap, the sampled size ceiling, the ptrace and seccomp assumptions around the in-memory receipt key).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-swarm-slices`>>
!! Row 7's slices ~ order, state and the design of S3 to S6

The wiki-daemon key cap stands in six slices, each red-first with its control. The operator ruled it at standing (2026-11-04) and ruled the ward's reach (2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/rulings`>>). The founding waits on `S1` to `S3`, `S2b`, `S3-pre-b` and `S3b`; `S4` to `S6` follow the standing.

|! Slice |! Holds |! State, 2026-11-05 |
| `S1` | the swarm tiddler model and the hand-only verbs | built; rides the `S2` lane |
| `S2` | the ward: a `ward-slots.bin` tree under each face, a fresh unlock per act, passkey refused | built with its cold-read cures and the marker cure (the product founds no face from a marker file); on its lane, CI green at its earlier head |
| `S2b` | the browser root under the ward | owed before the rite |
| `S3a` | the co-sign judge, the self-answer cure, `ask --detail`, the blinded per-face vessel tag over a random per-face tag secret (a D row, vessel-local, derived from no seed) | built on its own lane |
| `S3-pre-b` | the founding and join wiring of the vessel tag, default `required`: the founding mints the founder face's tag secret under its ask and its settle moves it onto the group's row; the join request mints the joinee face's | building on the `S3a` lane; founding-critical |
| `S3b` | the gate hook that calls the judge, the readers that hold each act to its outcome, and the co-signer's own ward | built on its own lane (`ward-cosign-gate`): the kāpae lower (node store row, node panel, browser), the house cap and the push go run through it; the board's fold, the cap's fold and the go's reader read each act only beside its spent co-sign, frozen at its outcome; a go names the go it follows and the branch's go ends its longest co-signed chain; a replay after a crash runs nothing; the co-signer's approve opens its own ward and carries that ward's per-face key's signature over (the answer, the ask's digest, the frontier it read), the key riding the device's admission; the outcome cites the answer's frontier; the browser answers through its own ward; and the answer door refuses an ask it cannot see or one already spent |
| `S4` | the capability channel and the AF_UNIX probe | designed below |
| `S5` | vessel-signed roster and receipt, the broker swap | designed below |
| `S6` | canon | designed below |

!!! Live holes the scouts found

Each cure rides the slice named.
* `S3`: the intent plane approves a SELF-answer. Any enrolled device signs an answer, and the read checks only that the answer's executor equals the acting atom, so the asker approves its own ask.
* `S4`: `tools/launch-codex.mjs` hands the supervisor fds 3 and 4 without close-on-exec, and the bwrap spawn probably carries them into bwrap and Codex. Treat it as red until a witness measures that the seat sees only fds 0 to 2.
* A same-uid process can open `session:open` with hand `cli` and wear the unlocked face (the connector defaults the hand to `cli`, and `cli` maps to the `verb` profile, which reaches every hand-only act, since the socket refuses hand-only acts for the `seat` profile alone). The ward closes that for bound acts. The lift posture for user wikis stays advisory by ruling.
* Node has no `socketpair(2)` and cannot pass an fd to an unrelated live process.
* `S3` (road `L24`), the renewal writer: a joinee seated with its tag that lost its answer and met a lease roll refused `replay-edge-lapsed` on its retry and had no way to boot. The join answer now reads the seated edge off the fold (the latest verified `device-edge` act for that device) and, where the lease rolled past it, the root appends one `device-edge` act for the seated admission at the current epoch and serves that edge; the answer serves what the log holds, and a request whose cached answer the log no longer seats still refuses `replay-edge-lapsed`. It never appends a second admission and never moves the tag, and a device the fleet set aside refuses `DeviceSetAside` ahead of it. Vector: `device-admit-edge-renewal.test.ts`, on the real KEL, fold, lease slot and payload producer. A roll itself still strands every booted device, the rolling vessel's own among them, at its next boot: each one's sealed anchor pins the edge digest it landed, and only founding, admission and recovery move that pin. That law stands declared in the red register (`A LEASE ROLL LEAVES EVERY SEATED DEVICE ABLE TO BOOT`) and waits on the operator's ruling over who moves the pin at a roll.
* `S3` (road `L24`), the lease guard (house ruling 2026-11-05; the operator may overrule it): until the renewal at the roll stands, `lares persona lease` refuses `lease-roll-strands-devices` (`LeaseRollStrandsDevices`) before it signs or writes, whenever this vessel's own edge binds the epoch the roll would leave or the face's `@persona` log seats another device's edge; the log carries an edge's digest and never its bound epoch, so every other seated edge counts. The refusal names the count and this owed row, and the CLI prints it. The ruling it records: a pin that moves to a renewed edge the root signed, that the log holds as the atom's latest `device-edge` act at a newer epoch, reads as a device-local cache update, not an identity change; the renewal at the roll builds after standing under that reading. Vector: `persona-lease-verb.test.ts`, on the production verb, the real persona KEL and a real log.
* `S3`, the floor-door refusal: the floor key's door (`vesselFloorKeyDoor`) refuses every `vessel-face-tag*` name beside the sovereign gate bundle's, so no later call derives a vessel-wide tag secret that would link this vessel's faces; the registry keeps both tag names for the per-face key. Vector: `gate-bundle-custody.test.ts`; the floor gate bundle still derives. A founding killed between the tag's mint under its ask and its settle onto the group's row resumes on the same tag and settles one group row (`founding-seed-crash.test.ts`, a pin: it greened with no cure).

!!! S3 ~ the co-sign

* A pure judge, `judgeCosign({view, intentId, requester, projection, standing, digest})` in `lararium-mesh/src/ward-cosign.ts`. The device count reads the projection's signed admission fold: zero or one device answers `none` (the fresh unlock alone), two or more answer `cosign`.
* A co-sign approves only when the answer carries a signature by an enrolled device that differs from the requester and whose standing reads `stands`; the ask's signer equals the requester; the ask's detail equals the digest of (act, face, wiki, head cid); the answer stands uncontested; and the intent stands unspent. Set-aside devices count toward the total and never co-sign. The co-signing device fresh-unlocks too: its approve of a ward act signs only inside a fresh unlock of its own ward for the face (`signIntentAnswer` in `lararium-node/src/commands/intent.ts`), opened from its own route for that act and zeroed after, a passkey never opening it. A joinee holds no face root, yet its ward stands from its join: the join request founds the face's custody, and that founding binds the ward beside it. The store door runs `intent-answer` first with no route and asks one only where the signer names `ward-needs-fresh-unlock`; a deny, and an answer to any other ask, ask none. The answer door signs no answer to an ask this vessel cannot see (`ward-ask-unseen`: nothing tells it whether the ask names a ward act) and none to a ward act whose outcome already spent it (`ward-ask-spent`).
* THE WARD ON THE WIRE (house default pending the operator). Each device's admission into a face (the founding admission and the fleet-join request alike) carries its per-face ward key (`wardKey`) under the tag key's signature over (group, atom, ward key); beside it rides `wardPop`, that ward key's own signature over (group, atom, ward key) under `ward-key-pop` (`wardKeyPopImage`, `wardKeyPopHolds`), so the device proves it holds the key it names. The fold refuses `ward-key-absent`, and `ward-key-unproven` where the pop fails or stands absent, and keeps each seated device's key (`wardKeys`); a join request with none refuses `request-ward-key-absent`, one whose pop fails `request-ward-key-unproven`, and the hearth's admit refuses `ward-key-unproven` before its root signs. The pop binds the atom, which each face mints fresh per device, so it needs no nonce, and the fold reads it off the admission alone. A node's tag door signs the pop inside one fresh unlock of the face's ward from the act's route (the founding's own, or the one the joinee's face custody is born under, else the person at the terminal, else `ward-needs-fresh-unlock`); a browser's door signs with the seed its mint holds where that ask mints the key, and where the key stood, inside the per-act ward worker from the passphrase typed for it (a passkey refusing `ward-passkey-refused`). A node derives the ward signing seed from the face's own ward key (HKDF under `ward-sign-seed`; the ward key mints at random per face at the founding or the join), writes its public half to the face's `face-ward-key` D row as the ward founds or rewraps (THE REWRAP KEEPS THE KEY: a rewrap over a standing ward keeps its key, and one from an absent ward over a face whose key row stands refuses `ward-key-would-rotate`, since that ward founded the key the admission names and its sealed rows stay sealed under the missing tree), and signs through `WardSeat.wardSign` inside the fresh unlock; a browser mints a random seed per face in its `ward-sign/<group>` keystore row, which the per-act ward worker opens from the passphrase typed for that act (`browserWardSignRow`). No seed and no vessel-wide key derives either, so two faces of one vessel present ward keys no reader links. An approve carries `frontier` (the fleet frontier head the answerer read) and `wardSig`, that key's signature over (the answer, the ask's digest, the frontier) under `ward-answer` (`wardAnswerImage`, `buildWardedAnswer`). The judge counts an approve only where `wardSig` verifies under the answerer's admitted ward key (else `ward-answer-unwarded`), the gate's outcome cites the frontier the answer names, and the witness reads an outcome only where it cites that very frontier beside a holding ward signature. The node's answer door refuses an approve with no known frontier (`ward-cosign-frontier-unknown`); the browser's answer door (`browserWardAnswer`, the vessel's `ward-answer` verb) approves through its ward, a passkey route refusing `ward-passkey-refused`, and a deny opens no ward on either. The browser's ward act rides the keystore's own tree until `S2b` moves it under a ward key of its own, and it rides that ward's ceremony once it stands.
* `ward-kapae-lower`, `ward-house-cap` and `ward-push-go` register as single-effect face intent verbs with audience `asks`. The outcome slot spends the intent.
* The gate (an `S2` ward act) calls the judge. When a co-sign applies, it writes the ask and refuses `ward-awaiting-cosign: <ask cid>`: no clock, no timeout, no fallback. An offline second device refuses for good until it answers.
* The gate stands as `wardCosignGate` in `lararium-mesh/src/ward-cosign-gate.ts`, inside the doors themselves: `promoteBoardDoor`'s lower and `swarmCommand`'s cap and go each read the face's intent plane through a `cosign` port the vessel composes at the act (`nodeWardCosignPort` on a node, `browserWardCosignPort` in a browser). It reads through `judgeCosign` alone and never through `mayAct`. The act's intent takes the act's digest as its nonce, so one act at one head names one intent: the first reach issues it with its issuer-signed ask and refuses, a retry names the same ask and writes nothing, an approved act runs and then signs the outcome that spends it, and a replay refuses `ward-cosign-spent`. Every other judge refusal refuses `ward-cosign-<reason>`. A lower names the edge's standing heads and refuses `kapae-heads-moved` where they move under the answer; a cap names its value and the line it extends. The lane share rides the fresh unlock alone. Vectors: `ward-cosign-table.ts`, one table that the mesh, node and browser (chromium) rows run over the real doors and a real intent document, with a witness that the gate calls the judge and no `mayAct`.
* The readers hold every act to its outcome, never the door alone. A witness over the face's intent plane (`wardActWitness` in `lararium-mesh/src/ward-cosign.ts`) answers each reader: the promote board's fold reads a lower, the swarm board's fold a cap and `readGo` a go only where the face's signed admission fold holds zero or one device, or beside a spent co-sign. The spend stands FROZEN at its outcome: the gate's outcome cites the answer it spent and the fleet frontier head it read, and the witness judges that cited answer at that frontier (an answer to the issuer's own ask naming the act's digest, an approve naming the issuer as executor, from another device of the face, and both that device and the issuer standing at the cited frontier: no raise it counts sets either aside). What lands after the outcome, a second approve, a deny, a raise the next frontier counts, never changes what stands, so a landed cap never falls back to an older one; the judge reads any outcome the issuer signed as spent. The port reads the frontier head (`frontier()`, null where it does not read known) and a device's standing at one link of the fleet's chain (`stoodAt`, over `fleetFrontierLinks` and `fleetStoodAt` in `fleet-frontier.ts`), on node and browser alike, and the links read on while the head waits on an uncounted raise. An act the root signed outside the door, or an approved intent with no outcome, refuses `ward-act-uncosigned`; a fold that cannot be read stands no lower, so the brake holds. Every vessel composition hands its readers the witness over the same port its doors hand the gate; the always-accepting check the doors use for their own replay search (`ward-door-decided.ts`) stays out of the package index, and the node's custody fixture seam (`seatFaceAutoFounder`) rides the test-only entry `@lararium/node/testing`.
* A go names the go it follows at its branch (`after`: that go's cid as `show` reads it, null for the branch's first), never a clock or a counter: a fresh go for one head names a new intent and asks afresh, and a replay of an approved go names the spent one and refuses `ward-cosign-spent`. Each go rests at its own row (`go/<branch>/<go cid>`), and the branch's standing go ends the longest co-signed `after` chain from its first go (`readGoChain`): an older go written again ends a shorter chain and never stands, a go chained onto an orphan stands on no chain, and two tips read as a fork (`forked`) where neither stands until a fresh go names one. At the door a go naming an `after` a co-signed go already follows refuses `swarm-go-forked`, and one naming no tip refuses `swarm-go-after-moved`, both before the gate asks. `show` names `after` only from the standing tip, and a fork's tips as `forks`.
* The effect lands first and its outcome after it, for each act: an outcome written first would strand the act, since the same words (the same heads, the same cap line, the same `after`) name a spent intent with nothing landed. The readers read the act only beside its outcome, so a crash between the two leaves it unread; each effect finds its own earlier run by the act's digest, so the replay signs nothing, lands the owed outcome and refuses `ward-act-already-run`.
* Residuals, named: the admission's ward key stands as the house's default and waits on the operator's ruling over those identity bytes. A browser page holding its keystore unlocked reads the face's ward seed row by the vessel's own doors (the `S2b` residual the browser root shares), and the browser's mint signs its pop with the seed the page minted; `B1`'s custody move (slice 3) closes both, taking the keystore's rows off the page. A device whose ward tree goes missing (a node) or whose ward key row goes missing (a browser) holds no key its admission names, so its approves stop verifying until the face re-founds or the device is admitted again under a fresh face atom; no rotation act stands, since the root rows that would sign one stay sealed under the missing ward. An act signed while the face held one device reads refused once a second device stands (it fails toward the brake: the hand lowers or sets it again under the co-sign). The co-signer's answer carries the frontier it read, so an answer signed before a raise that set its signer aside still cites the link it read; the raise binds what follows it, never an answer already signed. A go chain lives in the device-local daemon wiki, so a writer that deletes its newer rows rolls the chain back; no reader holds the chain's history past its rows. A newer act whose cited link has not reached a reader reads unread there, and on the cap line the older cap stands until the link syncs.
* The device count counts vessels, never atoms: each admission carries the vessel's blinded tag for that face, and the fold refuses a second admission whose tag already stands. The tag rides a random tag secret the device mints per face, at the founding or at the join request, in that face's own D row (`vessel-face-tag`, `bundle: stays`); no seed derives it, so a thief holding the place seed links no two faces, and a re-join on one face presents the same tag. Reds: two faces' tags read apart from any place-seed child, the row sorts D and stays, a re-join re-presents its tag; control: another face tags apart, and the seed census reads any reach named `vesselFaceTagSecret` open.
* Reds: a self-answer must not approve; a digest mismatch, an aside answerer and a contested pair refuse; a replay after the outcome refuses `spent`; with two devices and a peer that never answers, a thousand retries stay `ward-awaiting-cosign`; on two devices a lower, a cap and a go the root signed outside the door read `ward-act-uncosigned`; on three devices a second approve or a deny after the outcome, and a co-signer set aside or reading `unknown` after it, leave the landed act standing; an outcome citing a frontier that counts its own issuer aside stands nothing; an answer to an ask the vessel cannot see refuses `ward-ask-unseen`, and one to a spent ward act `ward-ask-spent`; a go rollback, a fork and an orphan chain never stand, and a second go naming a followed `after` refuses `swarm-go-forked`; the product indexes export neither the always-accepting check nor the custody fixture seam; a fresh go for a gone head asks afresh; a crash between an effect and its outcome replays to `ward-act-already-run` with one effect; a co-signer's approve with no fresh unlock refuses `ward-needs-fresh-unlock`; an approve its atom alone signed, or one warded under another device's admitted key, refuses `ward-answer-unwarded` and stands no outcome; an issuer outcome citing an older frontier than its answer carries stands nothing; two faces of one vessel present one ward key (node and browser); the browser holds no answer door, and a passkey route on it refuses `ward-passkey-refused`; a gated row that the panel answers with a thrown `ward-needs-fresh-unlock` asks its route and runs again. Controls: a second device approves, a one-device fleet lands on the unlock alone and reads an act on its own, a replay of an approved go refuses spent, an approve under the co-signer's own route lands, the issuer set aside after a door-made outcome leaves the act standing, an approved intent with no outcome reads nothing, and a straight go chain stands at its tip alone. A table-driven case runs over node and browser vessels.

!!! S4 ~ the capability channel

* The swarm verbs form their own `swarmDoor` and never join the session acts the custody socket admits, so a session of any hand (`cli`, `tab`, `seat`) that reaches for a swarm act refuses `act-rides-the-custody-door`.
* A ward act, `swarm-holder-start {kind: broker|watchdog}`, makes the daemon spawn the holder from a pinned tool path with `stdio[3]="pipe"` (libuv's socketpair) through an `sh` double fork, so the holder reparents to init: the holder stands beside the daemon, never under it. A ppid-chain witness proves the daemon pid sits in no seat's or holder's ancestry, with a direct-child spawn as its red control. Only if that witness cannot hold does a daemon-minted bearer ride the broker's inherited pipe, zeroed after use.
* The daemon identifies a holder by which end of the pair spoke, and no token rides a frame. The broker holds go-read, go-spend, receipt-sign, receipt-verify and cap-read; the watchdog holds roster-write alone. An EOF or a dead daemon reads `cap-gone`, and a restart mints fresh pairs.
* The probe measures first. A fixture server (never the daemon) takes a connect under bwrap plus Codex's sandbox with the network off and workspace write (`codex sandbox linux` or `codex debug landlock`; no model call). `tools/seat-socket-probe.expect.json` pins the measured result, and the hermetic arms assert both outcomes: a connect that succeeds without a capability gets `no-cap`, and a denied connect gets EPERM. Until the probe measures, a Codex seat counts as able to reach the socket, so the verbs ride the fd and never the face-wearing socket path.
* The fd cure closes fds 3 and up before `exec bwrap`.

!!! S5 ~ the broker swap

* A client over the inherited capability fd, `tools/swarm-door.mjs`, carries the frames: `go-read` (the daemon verifies the hand's `wardSig` and the co-sign outcome, spends once and returns branch and head), `receipt-sign` (a vessel signature in the domain `swarm-receipt`, writing `receipt/<tree>`), `receipt-verify`, `cap-read` and, for the watchdog, `roster-write`. A locked custody answers `swarm-vessel-locked`.
* The flat go file, the relay's HMAC key, its receipts map and the go lane directory retire (`push-go.mjs` goes; the isolated state stays). With no capability fd the relay refuses at start, before it makes a broker root, with "no standing daemon: swarm controls need the founded wiki", and writes nothing. The watchdog stops a seat on a capability EOF, and the ceiling still rides the launch argument.
* Reds, against a stub daemon pair end: a push whose go came only from the stub pushes (control); no record gives "no push-go"; a wrong head, a replay after the spend and a forged receipt all refuse; with no fd the relay exits non-zero and the broker root shows nothing new. A retirement witness (`tools/swarm-retired-witness.sh`) greps for `push-go`, `createHmac`, a `randomBytes(32)` key in the relay, the go file and the go lane directory, with a positive fixture. On the node side, a receipt signature verifies under the vessel's public key, and a go that was never warded answers `go-unsigned`.
* The residual: the receipt trust root names the daemon's vessel key.

!!! S6 ~ canon

This carrier gains its standing sections and rewrites the broker bullets and the trust-model line that name an in-memory key; the first owed row's in-memory-key residual retargets to the vessel-key residual; `kahua-memory-stage` closes the lift tension for the daemon and kahua wikis once the ward stands and keeps it open for user wikis; the road's `L22` row and the `F11` note follow.

